## 2 How To Read This Guide (What Actually Matters)

Marketing spec sheets bury the few things that decide whether you'll be happy. In order of importance for most beards:

1. **Length control.** The gap between guide-comb lengths determines how much you can fine-tune. A dial with 0.5 mm steps (or a precision guard) beats a handful of snap-on combs if you care about a specific length.
2. **Blade quality and self-sharpening.** Steel self-sharpening blades that never need oil are the modern standard; they stay sharp for years and avoid the tug-and-pull that makes cheap trimmers uncomfortable.
3. **Motor power vs. beard density.** Thin/medium beards are easy; thick, coarse, or long beards bog down weak motors. Premium units add sensors that boost power in dense patches so the blade doesn't stall or pull.
4. **Battery and charging.** Look for lithium-ion, a usable runtime (60 min+), and a quick-charge mode. Cordless-only is fine for most; cord/cordless is handy if you forget to charge.
5. **Wet/dry + washability.** Fully washable heads (rinse under the tap) are far easier to keep hygienic; wet/dry lets you trim in the shower.
6. **Attachments you'll actually use.** More heads = more versatility, but a giant kit is wasted if you only ever trim a beard. Match the kit to your real routine.

